meta {
  name: Upload image (product or label)
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/products/upload-image
  body: multipart-form
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{accessToken}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  image: @file()
  kind: product
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 200) {
    const body = res.getBody();
    if (body.imageKey) {
      bru.setEnvVar('productImageKey', body.imageKey);
      console.log('productImageKey set to', body.imageKey);
    }
    // For kind=product the response also carries front-of-pack suggestions.
    if (body.name || body.brand || body.genericName || body.category) {
      console.log('photo suggestions', {
        name: body.name,
        brand: body.brand,
        genericName: body.genericName,
        category: body.category,
      });
      if (body.category) {
        bru.setEnvVar('suggestedCategory', body.category);
      }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Multipart upload. The image is run through an AI plausibility / abuse gate (P5-005)
  BEFORE being stored, so a rejected image is never persisted.

  Success (200):
    - kind=product → { imageKey, name, brand, genericName, category } — `imageKey` is the S3
      object key (processed/{uuid}.jpg) of the eventual resized image; the server
      resolves keys to public URLs (ASSET_BASE_URL) when serving products.
      name/brand/genericName are front-of-pack suggestions read off the photo (any
      may be null). `category` (TICKET-P6-003) is a guess at one of
      `constants/categorySeeds.ts`'s seed slugs, or null — re-validated against that
      list server-side regardless of mode, so it's always either a known slug or null.
      The post-response script stores `imageKey` as productImageKey and `category` as
      suggestedCategory so Submit product can reuse both.
    - kind=label   → { imageKey } only.
    - kind=item (TICKET-P6-007, custom products — a cocktail, a dish, loose-leaf tea)
      → { imageKey, name } only. The plausibility prompt for this kind asks "is this a
      real thing a person could have consumed, photographed in good faith" rather than
      "is this packaging" — a cocktail in a glass passes. There is no brand/genericName
      suggestion: a custom product has no brand and no packaging-derived category. The
      resizer caps `item` at 1200px, same as `product`. See Create custom product.

  Set the image field to a JPEG / PNG / WebP / GIF / TIFF / AVIF file (8 MB hard cap).
  Set kind to 'product', 'label', or 'item'.

  Requires a registered (non-anonymous) session — anonymous tokens get
  403 { error: 'Registration required' } before the plausibility check runs, so a guest
  can never trigger the paid Gemini call. Use "Sign in with password" to get a
  registered accessToken; "Sign in anonymously" will 403 here.

  Failure codes:
    - 400 (missing image / bad kind)
    - 403 (anonymous session — registration required)
    - 413 (over 8 MB)
    - 415 (unsupported format)
    - 422 { error: 'image_rejected', reason } — plausibility rejection (not a product /
      too blurry / abusive). Abusive uploads additionally record a server-side
      UserAbuseFlag; the client only ever sees a generic reason for those.
    - 503 { code: 'daily_quota_exhausted' } — GEMINI_DAILY_CALL_CAP reached for the day
      (ADR 0005 L2); the plausibility check never ran, so nothing was persisted.

  Note: with PLAUSIBILITY_MODE=mock (the local/test default) every image is accepted.
  Set PLAUSIBILITY_MODE=gemini (and GEMINI_API_KEY) to exercise the real gate.
}
